Q. What is React?
A. React is a JavaScript library for building component-based user interfaces.
  Its major concepts are:
  Components
  Props
  State
  Hooks
  Virtual DOM
  Unidirectional data flow
  Reusable component architecture
  Redux/Redux Toolkit or React Query

Q. What is JSX?
A. JSX is a syntax extension that allows us to write HTML-like code inside JavaScript.
    const element = <h1>Hello {name}</h1>;
    JSX is transformed into JavaScript calls during compilation.
    Important: JSX is not HTML. It follows JavaScript/React rules, such as className instead of class.

Q. What is the Virtual DOM?
A. The Virtual DOM is an in-memory representation of the UI.
    When state or props change:
    React creates a new virtual representation.
    React compares it with the previous representation.
    It determines what changed.
    React updates the required parts of the real DOM.

    This comparison process is part of reconciliation.

Q. What is the difference between props and state in React? Explain default props in React?
A. Props are inputs passed from a parent component to a child and should be treated as read-only.
    State is data managed by the component itself and can change over time.

    Props → passed by parent
    State → managed by component

    default props are fallback values used when a component does not receive a particular prop.
    function User({ name = "Guest", age = 18 }) {
      return (
        <div>
          <h2>{name}</h2>
          <p>Age: {age}</p>
        </div>
      );
    }

  Props	                        State
  Passed from parent	          Managed by component
  Read-only	                    Can change
  Used to configure component	  Used for dynamic data
  Parent controls them	        Component controls them

Q. What is prop drilling?
A. Prop drilling occurs when data is passed through multiple intermediate components just to reach a deeply nested component.
  Even if Parent, Child, and GrandChild don't need the data, they have to pass it.
  Solutions include:
  Context API
  Redux/Zustand
  Component composition
  Custom hooks
  Server-state libraries where appropriate

Q. What is component composition?
A. Composition means building complex components by combining smaller components rather than creating a large inheritance hierarchy.
    Example:
    <Card>
      <UserProfile />
    </Card>    
    The Card component doesn't need to know what is inside it.
    Strong point: React generally favors composition over inheritance.

Q. What are controlled and uncontrolled components?
A. Controlled : React controls the input value.

  const [name, setName] = useState("");
  <input
    value={name}
    onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)}
  />

  Uncontrolled : The DOM maintains the value.

  const inputRef = useRef();
  <input ref={inputRef} />
  Controlled components are commonly preferred for complex forms because React has full control over the form state.

Q. What are React Hooks?
A. Hooks allow functional components to use React features such as state, context, refs, and effects.
  Common hooks:
    useState
    useEffect
    useContext
    useReducer
    useRef
    useMemo
    useCallback
    useLayoutEffect

  useState adds local state to a functional component.
    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
    setCount(count + 1);  // calling 

  React schedules a state update and may re-render the component.
  For updates based on previous state, I prefer the functional form:
    setCount(prev => prev + 1);
  This avoids relying on a potentially stale value.


  useEffect is used to synchronize a component with external systems such as:
    API calls
    subscriptions
    timers
    browser APIs
    event listeners

    useEffect(() => {
      fetchUsers();
    }, []);
  The empty dependency array means the effect doesn't depend on changing reactive values.
  Important interview point: 
  useEffect should not be treated as a general-purpose place for every piece of component logic. 
  If something can be calculated during rendering, an effect may be unnecessary.


  useMemo memoizes the result of an expensive calculation.
  const filteredUsers = useMemo(() => {
    return users.filter(user => user.active);
  }, [users]);
  The calculation is recomputed when dependencies change.
  Important: I don't use useMemo everywhere. It has its own overhead and should be used when memoization actually helps.


  useCallback memoizes a function reference.
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log("clicked");
  }, []);
  It's particularly useful when passing callbacks to memoized child components or when function identity matters.


Q. What are the rules of Hooks?
A. Two major rules:
    Call Hooks only at the top level.
    Call Hooks only from React components or custom Hooks.

  useEffect(() => {
    if (isLoggedIn) {
      // logic
    }
  }, [isLoggedIn]);

Q. Difference between useMemo and useCallback.
A.   useMemo     → memoizes a calculated value
      useCallback → memoizes a function

Q. When should you NOT use useMemo or useCallback?
A. I don't use them by default.
  If the calculation is cheap or the child isn't sensitive to function identity, memoization can make the code more complex without providing meaningful benefit.
  I first identify an actual performance issue using profiling, then optimize.

Q. What is the dependency array in useEffect?
A. useEffect(() => {
    console.log(userId);
  }, [userId]);
  The effect runs when userId changes.

  Three common forms:
  useEffect(() => {});  // Runs after every relevant render.
  useEffect(() => {}, []);  // Runs after initial mount, subject to development Strict Mode behavior.
  useEffect(() => {}, [userId]);  // Runs when userId changes.

Q. What is cleanup in useEffect?
A. Cleanup is used to remove subscriptions, timers, listeners, etc.
  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      console.log("Running");
    }, 1000);
  
    return () => {
      clearInterval(timer);
    };
  }, []);
  Without cleanup, we can create memory leaks or duplicate subscriptions.

Q. How do you cancel an API request in React?
A. I use AbortController
  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();  
    fetch("/api/users", {
      signal: controller.signal
    });  
    return () => controller.abort();
  }, []);
  This is especially useful when a component unmounts or the request becomes obsolete

Q. What is a stale closure?
A. A stale closure happens when a callback captures an old value from a previous render.
  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      console.log(count);
    }, 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(timer);
  }, []);
  Here the callback can keep referring to the initial count.
  Possible solutions include:
    Correct dependencies
    Functional state updates
    useRef when appropriate

Q. What is React.memo?
A. React.memo prevents a functional component from re-rendering when its props are considered unchanged.
    const User = React.memo(({ name }) => {
      return <div>{name}</div>;
    });
  However, if an object/function is recreated every render, shallow comparison can still detect a different reference.
  That's where stable references from useMemo or useCallback may help.

Q. What causes a React component to re-render?
A. Common causes include:
    Its state changes
    Its parent re-renders
    Its context value changes
    Its subscribed external store changes
    Its props change
    A parent re-render doesn't necessarily mean the DOM will be changed. React still performs reconciliation.

Q. How do you optimize a slow React application?
A. I generally follow this process:
    Profile the application.
    Identify unnecessary renders.
    Split large components.
    Use React.memo where appropriate.
    Use useMemo/useCallback selectively.
    Virtualize large lists.
    Lazy-load large components.
    Optimize API calls.
    Cache server state.
    Reduce unnecessary context updates.
    Optimize images/assets.
    Analyze bundle size.
    
    I would avoid premature optimization.


